ook on c1.","The pawn on h5 can be taken by the g6 pawn.","Better was 21.Rxc8 (−0.18), which takes the rook on c8."],"better":"21.Rxc8 trades rooks. Engine line: 21…Qxc8 22.Qd2 h5 23.f3 Qd8 24.b4 (−0.18)."},{"ply":42,"name":"21…Nf5","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"21…Nf5 misses the chance 21.h5 gave: 21…Rxc1 would have punished it. Black's winning chances go from 73% to 59%.","points":["White answers 22.Rxc8, which takes the rook on c8.","Better was 21…Rxc1 (−2.71), which takes the rook on c1."],"better":"21…Rxc1 trades rooks. After 22.Rxc1 Nf5 23.Bf4 Qd5 24.Qf3 Qxd4 25.Nc6 Bxc6 26.Rxc6 Nh4, Black comes out a pawn up (−2.71)."},{"ply":49,"name":"25.Nxg6","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"25.Nxg6 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 49% to 21%.","points":["Black answers 25…fxg6, which takes the knight on g6.","After fxg6, Black wins 2 points of material within 3 moves.","Better was 25.Nxf7 (−0.14), which takes the pawn on f7."],"better":"25.Nxf7 wins a rook for 6 points of material. After 25…Rxf7 26.Bxe6 Bd5 27.Bxf7+ Kxf7 28.Be5, White comes out a pawn up (−0.14)."},{"ply":52,"name":"26…Bd5","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"26…Bd5 lets the win slip: 26…Kh7 kept Black winning. Black's winning chances go from 80% to 64%.","points":["White answers 27.Bxd5, which takes the bishop on d5.","Better was 26…Kh7 (−3.73), which protects the g6 pawn, which was hanging."],"better":"26…Kh7 stops Re8+. After 27.Bd6 Re8 28.Bb4 Rxe6 29.Bxe6 Qg5, the rooks are traded (−3.73)."},{"ply":53,"name":"27.Rd6","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"27.Rd6 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 36% to 11%.","points":["Black answers 27…Nxd6, which takes the rook on d6.","The rook on d6 can be taken by the knight on f5 or the queen on d8.","After Nxd6, Black wins 2 points of material within 3 moves.","Better was 27.Bxd5 (−1.52), which takes the bishop on d5."],"better":"27.Bxd5 trades off the attacked bishop instead of losing it to …Bxa2. Engine line: 27…Qxd5 28.Rxg6 Nxd4 29.Rd6 Qc5 30.b4 (−1.52)."},{"ply":73,"name":"37.Be6","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"37.Be6 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 4% to 0%. It allows mate in 2.","points":["Black answers 37…Qb1+: the queen forks the king and the pawn: both can't be saved at once.","After Qb1+, Black wins 9 points of material within 3 moves.","Better was 37.Bc2 (−8.36), which attacks the queen."],"better":"37.Bc2 threatens Bxf5, winning the queen for a bishop, and stops the mate threat …Qb1+. After 37…Qg4 38.Qxg6+ Qxg6 39.Bxg6 Rxc7 40.Bxf7+ Kxf7 41.Kf1 Bxb2 42.g4, the queens come off and White is a piece down (−8.36)."}],"opening":{"name":"Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System","eco":"D00","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 5…b6: 10 moves in all, 5 by White and 5 by Black.","Theory runs out after 5…b6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}